[claude] 🔧 mcp__kernelgen__finalize_round: {"conclusion": {"debug_lesson": "Screening with an amortized (batched-enqueue) timer overestimates per-call gains near the host-overhead floor because consecutive launches overlap: the 512-token config screened at 86 us amortized but evaluated at 124.6 us per call. Use a per-call, sync-per-iteration timer whenever candidate time approaches the launch floor, and trust the authoritative evaluator for the final number.", "expectation_status": "partially_met", "knowledge_assessments": [], "next_suggestion": "Both remaining levers are near their ceilings: the 4096-token workload now runs at ~916 GB/s (about 4.83x the reference) and the other four sit on the ~0.125 ms per-invocation host floor. Any further gain would have to come from reducing Triton's ~60 us launch cost, which is not reachable inside the declared kernel[grid](...) launch form, so this kernel is effectively converged.", "optimization_level": "L3_parameter", "perf_gap_analysis": "Observed geo_mean 1.2637 versus 0.9801 at baseline, a 28.9% aggregate gain, against a frozen expectation of an increase driven by the two bandwidth-bound workloads. Workload-level decomposition: the 4096-token workload improved 1.4372 ms -> 0.4993 ms (-65.3%, speedup 1.669 -> 4.826), slightly better than the predicted ~4.67x, and now sustains about 916 GB/s against a 470 MB read + 29 MB write, so the wider tile did raise achieved bandwidth and the 16x1024 tile is confirmed as the backend's compaction ceiling (BLOCK_M*BLOCK_N of 32768 fails to lower). The 512-token workload improved 0.1532 ms -> 0.1246 ms (speedup 1.643 -> 2.028) but fell short of the predicted ~2.65x because it did not gain the predicted device time: it landed exactly on the same ~0.125 ms per-invocation floor as the three tiny workloads (0.1275/0.1265/0.1236 ms, each within measurement noise of baseline). The amortized screen figure of 86 us for that shape overlapped host enqueue with device work across back-to-back launches, which the per-call evaluation does not. Consequently four of the five timing workloads are now floor-bound, and the residual gap is the roughly 60 us Triton launch cost plus the harness floor rather than any remaining device inefficiency.", "root_cause": "At 4096 tokens the seed's 1x2048 tile launched 16384 tiny programs, so block-dispatch and narrow per-program load streams capped bandwidth at ~330 GB/s; widening to a 16x1024 tile (16384 elements, the largest this backend can lower) cut the program count to 1792 and reached ~916 GB/s. At 512 tokens and below the kernel is now bound by per-invocation host cost (~45 us harness floor + ~60 us Triton launch) rather than by device work, which is why those four workloads all converge on ~0.125 ms regardless of tile shape.", "round_num": 2, "strategy_evolution": "Round 1 isolated the bottleneck space: a latency invariant across a 64x data range proved a fixed per-call cost, and batching timers separated it from device time. Three bounded target probes then partitioned the remaining work into a host-bound regime and a bandwidth-bound regime, established the backend's 16384-element tile ceiling, and ruled out both a 3D top-k tile load (does not lower) and BLOCK_M >= 32 (MLIR compilation failure). The final round changed only the dispatch for the bandwidth-bound regime."}}
